<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gurugram</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gurgaon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gurugram, known until 2016 as Gurgaon, is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haryana">Haryana</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> and part of the National Capital Region. Once a small town and district headquarters, it grew from the 1990s into a major centre of finance, information technology and corporate offices. Its district includes the town of Pataudi, seat of the former princely state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gurugram.jpg" alt="Gurugram" width="320" height="155" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tarun4u · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cyber_City_View.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Gurugram lies in the south-eastern part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haryana">Haryana</a>, on the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>. It is about 30 kilometres south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-delhi">New Delhi</a> and 268 kilometres south of Chandigarh, the state capital. The city covers 333 square kilometres at an average elevation of 237 metres above sea level. It stands on the Sahibi River, a tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a> that rises in the Aravalli range, and its climate is hot and semi-arid, with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a> from late June to September. Mangar Bani, a sacred grove between Gurgaon and Faridabad, is among the last surviving natural forests of the National Capital Region and is protected by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gurjars">Gurjars</a> of the nearby area.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The region originally fell under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuru-kingdom">Kuru kingdom</a>, and in the late 4th century BCE it was absorbed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a> in the early expansion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandragupta-maurya">Chandragupta Maurya</a>. Gurgaon may be the Gudapura mentioned in the 12th-century Prithviraja Vijaya, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagarjuna">Nagarjuna</a>, a cousin of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prithviraj-chauhan">Prithviraj Chauhan</a>, rebelled and seized the town before the king recaptured it. Alexander Cunningham recorded a stone pillar of a local lord, Durgga Naga, with an inscription dated to 672 CE or 871 CE.</p><p>In the Mughal period and in the early British period Gurgaon was a small village in the Jharsa pargana of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> subah. The pargana passed to Begum Samru in 1776-77 and came under direct British rule in 1836 after her death, when the British set up a civil lines at Jharsa and a cavalry cantonment at Hiyadatpur. Gurgaon became a district in 1818 after the Bharawas district was disbanded. British-era buildings include the Church of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epiphany-holiday">Epiphany</a> and Kaman Serai, built in 1925, and the Gurgaon Club. On 12 April 2016 the chief minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haryana">Haryana</a>, Manohar Lal Khattar, proposed the name Gurugram, meaning village of the Guru, citing the city's association with Drona; the Union Government approved it on 27 September 2016.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Gurugram is the administrative headquarters of Gurgaon district and a satellite city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> within the National Capital Region. It had 876,969 inhabitants at the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, and Hindi was the first language of 74.18 per cent of them. The city is divided into 36 wards and 115 sectors. Its economy grew around the Maruti Suzuki plant and, later, outsourcing operations set up by General Electric with the developer DLF, and it adjoins the manufacturing and real-estate areas of New Gurgaon, Manesar and Sohna. The Sheetla Mata Mandir, dedicated to Kripi, the wife of Dronacharya, stands at the centre of the city.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gurgaon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pataudi acceded to India and in 1948 was merged into East Punjab; the town is now in <b>Gurugram</b> district of Haryana.</blockquote><a href="/state/pataudi-state">Pataudi State</a></li></ul></section>
